WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.520
Today's video is like nothing you ever bought at IKEA.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.620
It comes to you in only two parts.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.060
Part One: Commencing

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.360
So, over the weekend, Sarah and I drove to Gambier, Ohio

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.440
Where I got to deliver the commencement address

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.560
at my Alma mater, Kenyon College.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.860
Here's part of what I said:

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.540
So, when I was a student here, uh,

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.100
There was widespread agreement among my peers

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.780
That the so called "real world of proper adulthood"

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.860
was basically a disease you caught

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.260
and then eventually died from.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.340
(audience laughter)

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.360
Like, adulthood, with its mortgages,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.180
and spreadsheets and lawn maintenance seemed to be a thing

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.560
to be dreaded

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.260
and resisted, until finally, it overtook you

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.740
like a zombie plague.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.680
(slight audience laughter)

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.560
And then, like, once you acquired adulthood,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.540
you would start saying things like "brand awareness

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.760
in a fractured media landscape."

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.940
(audience laughter)

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.000
and "we need a president who knows how to get things done."

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.720
To be an adult meant engaging in totally unironic

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.880
conversations about the weather.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.460
(audience laughter)

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.260
I remember once when I was at Kenyon,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.860
my grandmother called me to tell me that she was

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.900
watching the Weather Channel and that it looked like it was raining

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:11.700
in Ohio

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.360
(audience laughter)

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.920
I explained to her that I was reading Ulysses

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.520
That I wasn't even in Gambier,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.400
but in Dublin, Ireland in 1904

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.580
and that history was a nightmare from which

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.520
Dedalus was trying to awake

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:25.520
and that nothing,

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.540
literally nothing

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.080
mattered less than the current weather.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.540
(audience laughter)

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.260
and then after a moment she asked,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.320
"Well, is it raining, or isn't it?"

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.440
(audience laughter)

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.680
To be an adult was to be a river rock

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.540
blasted by an endless torrent of mundane terrors,

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.320
from resume formatting,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.900
to electricity bills

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.320
that would inevitably smooth all my hard edges

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.920
until I looked and felt just like everything else.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.200
Now, this is the part of the commencement address

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.460
where I'm supposed to tell you that it in fact,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.360
adulthood isn't so bad and

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.100
blah, blah, blah,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.780
but no, no, it is so bad! (audience laughter)

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.700
side note: graduation gowns are not flattering.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.740
In the end, the speech is mostly just a celebration

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.800
of unironic conversations about the weather

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.440
If you want to watch or read the whole thing,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.800
you can do so. Links in the doobly doo.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.360
Part Two: sports.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.360
So, Hank, I'm in the midst of the greatest sports month of my life.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.220
A couple weeks ago, I drove the pace car

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.400
at the Indianapolis Grand Prix

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.300
which allowed me to experience what it's like to be

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.700
tailgated by thirty professional race car drivers.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.560
We're just six days away from the annual

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.140
highlight of my social calendar,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.500
the Indianapolis 500

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.560
and most amazingly, the fourth tier English

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.440
football team, Nerdfighteria sponsors

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.440
AFC Wimbledon have found themselves

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.200
in the playoff finals, just one victory away

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.660
from being a third tier English football team.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.120
That's right, Hank, on May 30th, AFC Wimbledon

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.880
will take the field at Wembley, a 90,000

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.400
seat stadium, and wearing DFTBA

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.840
on the back of their shorts,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.900
they will play a one game playoff against

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.980
The Sailing Ships of Plymouth Argyle

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.400
It's hard to describe how unlikely this is,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.280
Wimbledon have the smallest stadium of any team in their league,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.760
and among the smallest budgets.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.180
They'd also be the only English team owned by their fans

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.340
to be as high as the third tier,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.760
so this is a massive moment for the club,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.800
and, as you know if you listen to our podcast

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.760
where you can get all the week's news from both Mars and AFC Wimbledon

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.140
I am immensely excited!

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.940
In fact Hank, I am leaving directly from the Indianapolis 500

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.200
to go to the game in London.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.300
So Nerdfighters, if you live in or near London, I hope

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.260
you'll come to Wembley on May 30th to cheer on the Dons

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.220
If you want to go to the game but can't afford a ticket,

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.940
Email me and I will try to work something out

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.440
Info on all of that in the doobly doo

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.540
Hank, I know that sports are silly.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.600
But as Pope John Paul II once said:

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.380
"Of all the unimportant things, football is the most important."

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.020
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

